WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.520
Some movies begin setting up sequels during
the first film, promising even greater adventures

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.940
with characters audiences are just getting
to know.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:12.860
But sometimes, for a variety of reasons, this
doesn't always work out—and audiences are

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.560
left hanging, awaiting a next chapter that
will likely never happen.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:17.720
For example ...

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:19.240
Fantastic Four

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:24.740
Fox Studios' 2015 reboot of Fantastic Four
had a famously troubled production, and it's

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:29.960
been suggested by both cast and crew that
studio meddling kept director Josh Trank from

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:31.620
making the film he wanted to make.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:36.260
While the film had an interesting take on
the team's abilities and featured a stellar

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:39.480
cast, it was a resounding box office and critical
failure.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:44.360
Despite all of this, Fox insisted in the aftermath
they were going ahead with a sequel, even

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:49.180
setting a 2017 release date … before taking
it off the schedule three months after the

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:49.900
film's release.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:52.420
The film's ending certainly set up a sequel
...

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:56.320
"Because we're a team now, and there's four
of us, so we should come up with a name for

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:56.820
it."

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.239
… but for now, the screen fate of Marvel's
first family appears to be in limbo — a

00:01:02.239 --> 00:01:06.830
producer commented in early 2017 that Fox
would "not make another Fantastic Four movie

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:09.900
until it was ready to be made," which could
mean anything.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:11.400
John Carter

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:16.480
Attempts to adapt Edgar Rice Burroughs' classic
"Martian Series" of novels have been many

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:20.910
and varied, and they usually begin and end
in Development Hell.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:25.750
In 2009, rights holder Disney pinned their
hopes on writer and director Andrew Stanton,

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:30.220
the man at the helm of a few Pixar hits, who
had a grand vision for films based on the

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:31.960
first three books of the series.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:36.860
But audiences who had no idea who John Carter
was or why they should care stayed away in

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:42.960
droves, and the super-expensive flick contributed
to Disney taking an $84 million loss that

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:43.460
quarter.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.440
Despite the film's sequel-teasing ending and
Stanton's plans, Disney scrapped the entire

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:53.460
idea, even going so far as to allow the film
rights to revert back to Burroughs' estate.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:55.160
Masters of the Universe

00:01:55.660 --> 00:02:01.380
In 1985, the live-action adaptation of Masters
of the Universe, based on an insanely popular

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:05.960
He-Man toy line and animated series, was the
answer to every young boy's dream.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:11.280
Starring Dolph Lundgren, it looked guaranteed
to rake in the cash and spawn endless sequels,

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:16.240
and it seemed to confirm this with a very
early example of the post-credits teaser.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:20.689
Skeletor emerges from the pit of goo and tells
the audience in no uncertain terms that he'll

00:02:20.689 --> 00:02:23.000
be back—but it was not to be.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.590
The Cannon Films production was constantly
in danger of going broke, cuts were made wherever

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:32.080
they possibly could be, and the final fight
between He-Man and Skeletor takes place in

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:36.379
the dark for a reason—producers were literally
shutting down the production while cameras

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:37.040
were rolling.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:42.140
Despite this, production actually began on
a sequel, but it was scrapped and its sets

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:46.820
and costumes were recycled for the 1989 Jean-Claude
Van Damme flick Cyborg.

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:48.800
Freddy vs. Jason

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:55.060
Despite its numerous flaws, the 2003 crossover
movie Freddy vs. Jason pretty much gave fans

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:56.580
exactly what they came to see ...

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:59.120
"Are you crazy?

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.460
You got what you wanted, you pulled Freddy
out, and now he's fighting Jason.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:02.520
C'mon, what more do you want?"

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:06.660
The sight of the two towering titans of the
slasher genre beating the holy hell out of

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:12.120
each other helped to make up for any shortcomings,
and the film's ending left no doubt that Freddy—even

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.329
though he lost—wasn't done with Jason by
a long shot.

00:03:15.329 --> 00:03:19.939
But unfortunately, any of those fans still
waiting for a cinematic Round 2 are going

00:03:19.939 --> 00:03:22.040
to be waiting a long, long time.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:26.569
There are many reasons for this, including,
most notably, that New Line actually had to

00:03:26.569 --> 00:03:31.400
buy the rights to Friday the 13th in order
to get the crossover made, and Paramount long

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.040
ago reacquired those rights.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.200
So don't hold your breath, horror fans.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:36.640
Mac and Me

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:42.240
Although it came and went with practically
nobody noticing in 1988, Mac and Me has become

00:03:42.249 --> 00:03:44.340
infamous since the advent of YouTube.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:48.800
It has been called both the most brazen E.T.
ripoff ever and a 90-minute commercial for

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.080
McDonald's and Coke.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:54.540
Scenes like this show-stopping McDonald's
dance party and this ridiculous clip which

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:58.870
frequently shows up in compilations of the
very worst film scenes of all time elevate

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:02.999
the film from simple cash-in to some kind
of surreal capitalist trash masterpiece.

00:04:02.999 --> 00:04:07.909
The film ends with a "We'll Be Back" tease,
as the producers were obviously expecting

00:04:07.909 --> 00:04:13.120
the money to come rolling in, but the film
grossed only a measly $6.4 million.

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:14.820
Green Lantern

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:21.000
2011's Green Lantern was an ambitious, expensive,
and ultimately failed attempt by Warner Brothers

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:25.360
to make something happen with a DC property
that isn't Batman or Superman.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:30.070
But despite the casting of the ever-likable
Ryan Reynolds in the title role and a $200

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:35.700
million budget, the film was a complete mess,
the result of being plagued with bad decisions

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.960
and studio interference from the beginning.

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:42.820
The film's post-credits sequence hinted that
Sinestro would be the villain for the sequel,

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:47.410
but Green Lantern's critical and box office
performance forced Warner Brothers to rethink

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:52.850
their strategy, and a rebooted Green Lantern
film won't be arriving until 2020, by which

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:56.720
point Warner Brothers is hoping that fans
will have forgotten about this one …

00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:58.200
"Join me."

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:03.500
Thanks for watching!

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:06.380
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:08.710
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

